<h1 class="article-title">Liepāja SEZ Authority and Fokker Next Gen N.V. Sign a Memorandum of Cooperation</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>The memorandum of cooperation sets out the stages of a project to develop a new hydrogen-powered aircraft. These include establishing a centre of excellence in hydrogen aircraft operations engineering in Latvia, focusing on ground handling, airport infrastructure and hydrogen infrastructure.</p><p>For the development and manufacture of next-generation aircraft, Fokker Next Gen N.V. also intends to establish in Latvia a centre of excellence for hydrogen aircraft operations, focusing on ground handling, as well as a maintenance and documentation support centre to prepare documentation for airlines and aircraft maintenance facilities. The plans also include a final assembly line operating alongside the main assembly line in the Netherlands.</p><p>Fokker Next Gen N.V. recognises the advantages of the Liepāja SEZ and, through this memorandum, expresses its interest in conducting a feasibility study for a final assembly facility for new hydrogen-powered aircraft at Liepāja International Airport (LPX), which is within the Liepāja Special Economic Zone.</p><p>Fokker Next Gen N.V. CEO Juriaan Kellermann outlined the company’s plans: “The well-equipped Liepāja Airport, with extensive land suitable for our assembly line, is an excellent potential location for our facility. Its direct maritime connection with the Netherlands and expected proximity to future hydrogen production plants are significant advantages. The business-friendly environment in the region could also be highly favourable for our operations. We are excited about the opportunity to work with the Liepāja Special Economic Zone and other stakeholders to build a strong aviation ecosystem in Latvia. This initiative aims to support a possible final assembly line in Liepāja in the future, operating alongside the line in the Netherlands and significantly expanding our ability to manufacture innovative hydrogen-powered aircraft.”</p><p>Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis noted that Liepāja has historically been one of Latvia’s leading regional industrial centres, with rich traditions and experience. Over the last 25 years the city has built an integrated, high-quality business environment combining the port, manufacturing, airport, road and rail access, something appreciated by the Dutch company Fokker Next Gen N.V. Hmieļevskis added: “Liepāja also has a history of aircraft manufacturing. About a century ago, some 15 aircraft for the Latvian Aizsargi organisation were built at the Tosmare ship-repair plant, then known as the Naval Port Workshops. The aircraft were transported on rafts to the Liepāja Northern Airfield on the other side of the Karosta Canal for test flights. Liepāja can do it today, too.”</p><p>To advance such an ambitious project in Latvia, Fokker Next Gen N.V. CEO Juriaan Kellermann also signed a memorandum with Riga Technical University Rector Tālis Juhna, expressing an interest in jointly expanding research and innovation capabilities and developing a regional aviation innovation ecosystem, including efforts to attract national and international funding for education, research and innovation. Building a hydrogen aircraft operations engineering centre of excellence in Latvia will require drawing on existing scientific and educational expertise through high-level research, EU and other external funding, and commercialisation of research results so innovative technologies can reach the market. Developing highly skilled human capital is equally important.</p><p>The Dutch company Fokker Next Gen also signed a memorandum with Latvia’s Ministry of Economics, contributing to the development of a green-energy cluster on the Kurzeme coast. “Through this project, Fokker Next Gen will accelerate the development of hydrogen technologies. This is especially relevant in the context of the ELWIND offshore wind farm, as it could stimulate future demand for hydrogen fuel,” said Iveta Strupkāja, Acting Director of the Investment and Development Agency of Latvia, adding that several other companies have also expressed readiness to participate in the hydrogen cluster.</p></div>
